@page "/"
@using Restaurant.UI.Shared.Components.Screens

@* The terminal's only route, and a host rather than a screen. Everything it used
   to draw — the menu read, tap to add, the quantity merge on a repeat tap, the
   running total and the submit through CreateOrderAsync — now lives in
   OrderEntryScreen in Restaurant.UI.Shared, so the same component renders here
   in the MAUI WebView and in the back office's development preview. MAUI Blazor
   Hybrid is Razor in a WebView: one implementation, two hosts, and no second
   copy of the behaviour to keep in step.

   What this page owns is what a host owns: the route, the two literals that
   belong to this device rather than to the screen, and which destination is on
   screen. CustomerName and TableId keep the values the POC terminal has always
   posted; nothing on the screen collects either, and the floor plan that would
   pick a table is not built in this pass.

   The shell is TerminalShell — the 76px top bar and 78px bottom nav every
   terminal destination inherits (Part II-A). Two of its five destinations are
   built: ORDER, and MORE, which opens Printer setup.

   **Both destinations stay mounted and one is hidden.** The obvious form is an
   @@if that swaps them, and it loses the check: a server who taps MORE mid-order
   to find out why the label did not come out would come back to an empty cart,
   because unmounting OrderEntryScreen takes its lines with it. Those lines are
   component state and there is nowhere else for them to live until the offline
   queue exists (GAP-10), so the destination that is not on screen is hidden
   rather than destroyed. The wrapper is a plain block: TerminalShell's
   `.shell__body ::deep > *` already gives a destination flex:1 and the two
   min-0s, and it applies to whatever the direct child happens to be. *@

<TerminalShell ActiveDestination="@_destination" OnNavigate="Navigate" TerminalId="@TerminalId">
    <div style="@(_destination == "order" ? null : "display:none")">
        <OrderEntryScreen CustomerName="Mobile User" TableId="1" />
    </div>
    <div style="@(_destination == "more" ? null : "display:none")">
        <PrinterSetupScreen TerminalId="@TerminalId" />
    </div>
</TerminalShell>

@code {
    /// <summary>This terminal's id. It names the tablet in the shell's top bar and on
    /// the test label, so a label found on a bench says which device produced it.</summary>
    private const string TerminalId = "Counter 2";

    private string _destination = "order";

    /// <summary>
    /// The shell reports the tap and the host decides what is on screen. Only built
    /// destinations reach here — BottomNav renders the other three as spans rather
    /// than buttons — so this needs no allow-list of its own.
    /// </summary>
    private void Navigate(string key) => _destination = key;
}
